#EXTINF:4440 group-title="T",The Golden Bough Part 2 — James Frazer · IV — TABOOED PERSONS - PART 3. 6. Hunters and Fishers tabooed- Taboos observed by hunters and fishers probably dictated by a fear of the spirits of the animals or fish- Taboos observed as a preparation for whaling- fishing- and hunting- Taboos observed at the hatching and pairing of silkworms- Taboos observed by fishermen in Uganda- Taboos observed by hunters in Nias- Continence observed by fishers and hunters apparently based on a fear of offending the fish and animals- Chastity observed by American Indians before hunting- Taboos observed by Hidatsa Indians at catching eagles- Miscellaneous examples of chastity observed from superstitious motives- The taboos observed by hunters and fishers are often continued and increased in stringency after the animals and fish have been killed- Taboos observed by the Esquimaux after killing sea beasts- Native explanation of these taboos- Passage of animism into religion among the Esquimaux- The confession of sins originally practised as a kind of physical purgation- Possible survivals of savage taboos among civilised peoples- Purificatory ceremonies observed by hunters after slaying dangerous animals- such as panthers- lions- bears- and serpents Such purificatory ceremonies based on a fear of the souls of the animals. - Read by Leon Harvey - Duration 01:14:00
https://www.archive.org/download/goldenbough2_2109_librivox/goldenbough3_08_frazer_128kb.mp3
#EXTINF:3868 group-title="T",The Golden Bough Part 2 — James Frazer · V — TABOOED THINGS. PART 1. 1. The Meaning of Taboo. Taboos of holiness agree with taboos of pollution Because the savage does not distinguish between holiness and pollution- The principles of taboo to be further illustrated by tabooed things and tabooed words. 2. Iron tabooed. The bodies of kings not to be touched- especially with iron- The use of iron forbidden to kings and priests- Use of iron forbidden at circumcision- childbirth- and other rites and seasons- Use of iron forbidden in building- The taboo on iron perhaps based on its novelty- Everything new excites the fear of the savage- Iron used as a charm against demons and ghosts. 3. Sharp Weapons tabooed. Use of sharp weapons forbidden lest they wound spirits- Knives not used after deaths or funerals- Use of sharp weapons forbidden at pregnancy and childbirth. 4. Blood tabooed. Raw meat tabooed because the life or spirit is in the blood- Royal blood not to be spilt on the ground- Reluctance to shed any human blood on the ground- Unwillingness to shed the blood of animals- Sacredness of whatever is touched by a Maori chiePs blood- The juice of the grape regarded as the blood of the vine- Wine treated as blood and intoxication as inspiration- Men's dread of the blood of women. The Head tabooed. The head sacred on account of the residence of a spirit- Objection to have any one overhead- Sanctity of the head- especially of a childs head- in Polynesia and elsewhere. - Read by Leon Harvey - Duration 01:04:28
https://www.archive.org/download/goldenbough2_2109_librivox/goldenbough3_09_frazer_128kb.mp3
#EXTINF:3517 group-title="T",The Golden Bough Part 2 — James Frazer · V — TABOOED THINGS. PART 2. 6. Hair tabooed. Hair of kings- priests- and other tabooed persons kept unshorn- Hair kept unshorn on various occasions- such as a wife's pregnancy- a journey- and war- Hair unshorn during a vow- Nails of children not pared- Children's hair left unshorn as a refuge for their souls. 7. Ceremonies at Hair-cutting. Ceremonies at hair-cutting in Fiji- New Zealand- and Cambodia- Ceremonies at cutting the hair of Siamese children. 8. Disposal of Cut Hair and Nails. Belief that people may be bewitched through the clippings of their hair and the parings of their nails- Headaches caused by clipped hair- Rain- hail- thunder- and lightning caused by cut hair- Cut hair and nails used as hostages for the good behaviour of their original owners- Cut hair and nails deposited in sacred places- such as temples and cemeteries- Cut hair and nails buried under trees or deposited among the branches- Cut hair and nails stowed away in any safe place- Cut hair and nails kept against the resurrection- Cut hair and nails burnt to prevent them from falling into the hands of sorcerers- Hair-cutting as a purificatory ceremony to rid persons of the virus of taboo or the pollution of death. - Read by Leon Harvey - Duration 00:58:37
https://www.archive.org/download/goldenbough2_2109_librivox/goldenbough3_10_frazer_128kb.mp3
#EXTINF:3814 group-title="T",The Golden Bough Part 2 — James Frazer · V — TABOOED THINGS. PART 3. 9. Spittle tabooed. Belief that people maybe bewitched through their spittle- Hence precautions taken by persons- especially by chiefs and kings- to prevent their spittle from falling into the hands of sorcerers- Use of spittle in making a covenant. 10. Foods tabooed. Certain foods tabooed to sacred persons- such as kings and priests- These taboos probably based on the same motive which underlies the whole system of taboo.11. Knots and Rings tabooed. Knots and rings not worn by certain sacred persons- knots untied- locks unlocked- doors- etc.- opened- at childbirth to facilitate delivery- The crossing of the legs supposed to impede childbirth and other things- Knots supposed to prevent the consummation of marriage- Use of knots at marriage in Rotti- Knots used as charms to inflict or cure disease- Knots used as charms to win lovers or capture runaway slaves- Knots used as charms by hunters and travellers knots used as protective amulets in Russia and elsewhere- The magical virtue of a knot is that of an impediment for good or evil- Rule that the hair should be loose and the feet bare at certain rites- The custom of going on certain solemn occasions with one shoe on and one shoe off intended to free the man from magical constraint and to lay it on his enemy- Rings as magical fetters which prevent the egress or ingress of spirits- Rings as amulets against demons- witches- and ghosts- Why the Flamen Dialis might not wear knots and rings- The Gordian knot perhaps a talisman. - Read by Leon Harvey - Duration 01:03:34
https://www.archive.org/download/goldenbough2_2109_librivox/goldenbough3_11_frazer_128kb.mp3
#EXTINF:3774 group-title="T",The Golden Bough Part 2 — James Frazer · VI — TABOOED WORDS. PART 1. 1. Personal Names tabooed. The personal name regarded by the savage as a vital part of himself through which he can be magically injured- Personal names kept secret from fear of sorcery among the Australian aborigines- In Egypt- Africa- Asia- and the East Indies- And among the American Indians- Some savages- though they will not mention their own names- will invite others to do so for them- The prohibition to mention personal names is sometimes only temporary- In order to avoid the use of people's own names parents are sometimes named after their children- uncles and aunts after their nephews and nieces- etc. 2. Names of Relations tabooed. Prohibition to mention names of relations- especially of relations by marriage- Women's speech among the Caffres- Names of husbands- wives- first-born sons- etc.- Tabooed among various peoples- Names of relations- especially of relations by marriage- tabooed in the East Indies- In New Guinea- In Melanesia- And in Australia- These taboos not to be explained by the intermarriage of persons speaking different languages. - Read by Leon Harvey - Duration 01:02:54
https://www.archive.org/download/goldenbough2_2109_librivox/goldenbough3_12_frazer_128kb.mp3
#EXTINF:3093 group-title="T",The Golden Bough Part 2 — James Frazer · VI — TABOOED WORDS. PART 2. 3. Names of the Dead tabooed. Names of the dead not mentioned by the Australian aborigines- the American Indians- and other peoples- The taboo based on a fear of the ghosts- From a like fear namesakes of the dead change their names- Sometimes all the near relations of the deceased change their names- When the name of the deceased is that of a common object- the word is often dropped in ordinary speech and another substituted for it- Modification of savage languages produced by this custom- Historical tradition impeded by the custom- Revival of the names of the dead after a time- The dead supposed to be reincarnated in their namesakes- Names of the dead allowed to be mentioned after their bodies are decayed- Final mourning ceremony among the Arunta. - Read by Leon Harvey - Duration 00:51:33
